      e.g. 1812/index.html
      gate: write_entry_pages.mjs --self-test (check.sh) asserts the <base> is the first child of <head>, exact root-template copying, working year doors and the honest 1812 status; the deploy URL smoke (.github/chicago-4d-url-check.json) requests /4d/, /4d/1835/ and /4d/dev/1835/ live.
  1x transformed — publish.sh writes it each run from the same BUILD_VERSION and BUILD_CT as the visible stamp
      e.g. build.json
      gate: tools/test_dev_preview.mjs reads it. Until 2026-08-15 it was written ONCE by hand and never again — this gate found it two days stale, claiming a version the mirror beside it did not have. That is the exact fault class this file exists for, found on its first run.
  1038x transformed — gltf-transform meshopt derivative built by bake.sh from assets/gltf masters
      e.g. data/gltf/agency_striker_log_house__log_1830.glb
      gate: R-BUG3c-b: check.sh asserts the committed master against the heightfield and REPORTS the derivative's FIT; the renderer conforms the ground to the field at load, so the drawn surface is the sampler by construction. Its POSITION BIT DEPTH is asserted rather than reported (T-0012): tools/measure_terrain_fit.mjs --gate recovers the depth from the shipped rungs and fails if it is coarser than tools/web_derivatives.sh asks for, because the derivative gate next door compares triangles, node identity, materials and a bounding box within four rungs and a bit-depth change moves none of them. This is the entry that cost three parcels — read #145 before adding another. T-3232: a GLB that shares a texture sheet is also rewritten by tools/share_glb_sheets.mjs, its sheet moved to an external buffer; that rewrite is gated below and by check.sh, view for view against assets/web.
  54x transformed — tools/share_glb_sheets.mjs (run by publish.sh) writes each image two or more published GLBs embed once, named by its hash, and points those GLBs at it as an external buffer (T-3232)
      e.g. data/gltf/sheets/023ed50f69beaea7.ktx2
      gate: share_glb_sheets.mjs --check (here and in check.sh) reads every rewritten GLB back through its sheets and asserts its JSON, bufferViews and meshopt streams are its assets/web source's, each sheet is named by its own hash, and each is held by two or more GLBs.
  12x transformed — tools/prebuild_layers.mjs (run by publish.sh) writes the frontage layer's finished buffers, built in Node by the page's own createFrontage, with a hash of every file they were built from and the build id the page is stamped with, the buffer gzipped for the tree budget; the page inflates and uploads them instead of building them (T-2677); and the ground's tiles, cut into the page's 40 m pieces by terrain.js's and spatial-batch.js's own functions, uploaded in the ground GLB's place (T-2693); and the distant base, laid over frontage's crossing footprints and cut the same way, batched in place of the page's own grid and cut (T-2709); and the streets' surfaces laid by streets.js's own streetLayoutOf, one buffer per layout, batched in place of laying the road (T-2710); and the yards' five surfaces, laid by yards.js's own createFencedGround and cut the same way, uploaded in place of laying them (T-2711); and index.json, the list of every year and layer it wrote, which the page reads before asking for any of them (js/prebuilt-index.js), so a year with no prebuild costs no 404
      e.g. data/prebuilt/1835/base.bin.gz
      gate: this file refuses a prebuild whose inputs no longer hash to its index, or whose buffer is not the one its index hashed (prebuild_layers.mjs stale(), below); prebuild_layers.mjs --self-test (check.sh) proves two builds byte-identical and the page's replay of one equal to its own build; --parity reads the live page's buffers against it.
  1404x transformed — publish.sh writes the residents layer minified — the same JSON value on one line
      e.g. data/residents/attested_trade_houses.json
      gate: tools/check_published_residents.mjs asserts the SHIPPED file parses to a value deep-equal to its source, file for file, with none missing and none extra. The transform exists because the layer is 1,380 hand-annotated household records whose notes run to paragraphs: at indent=1 it was 8.8 MB of the 32 MB budget, the tree measured 31.999 MB on 2026-09-05, and the next resident pass could not land. Whitespace is the one thing in it a visitor never reads — the renderer fetches these with response.json().
